# =============================================================================
# Tooling caches, virtualenvs and editor state — machine-local, never shared
# =============================================================================
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
*.pyd
.env
.ipynb_checkpoints/
.venv/
venv/
# Linter/formatter/type-checker caches. `.ruff_cache/` was the gap: only the CACHEDIR.TAG inside it
# happened to be excluded, so the directory itself would have shipped.
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.pytest_cache/
.pyright/
CACHEDIR.TAG
# macOS / editor cruft
.DS_Store
.idea/
.vscode/
# Credentials / key material — never commit, even accidentally
*.pem
*.key
*.crt
*.p12
*.pfx
*.jks
credentials.json
token.json
.git-credentials

# Local `langgraph dev` state (pickled threads/runs/assistants) — machine-specific, never shared
.langgraph_api/
large_tool_results/

# Authoring notes and delivery material — useful while preparing a session, not part of the labs.
# Leading slash is REQUIRED where used: a bare `AGENTS.md` matches at any depth and would exclude
# day2/data/agent/AGENTS.md, which is workshop data the Deep Agent labs read at runtime.
# README.md is the only Markdown file that ships (see the `!README.md` negation below). It carries
# the full model-access content — both paths, setup, redaction, model tiers, troubleshooting — so
# nothing in the labs ever points at a document that is not in the repo.
#
# NOTE: uv.lock is deliberately NOT ignored. It used to be — orphaned under this comment block,
# which is about markdown authoring notes and has nothing to do with a lock file. Committing it is
# both the convention in the comparable LangChain repos (langgraph-101, lca-lc-foundations,
# langsmith-agent-lifecycle-workshop all track theirs) and what makes this repo's verification mean
# anything: the labs were proven against langchain 1.3.15 / langchain-openai 1.4.3, and without the
# lock `uv sync` resolves fresh and a participant can land on versions nobody tested.

# =============================================================================
# Documentation: README.md is the only doc in the repo
# =============================================================================
# Other root-level .md and .pdf files are authoring notes, kept out of the repo.
*.md
*.pdf
!README.md

# EXCEPTION — these are workshop DATA, not documentation. The labs read them at runtime:
#   day1/data/kb/            13 vendor KB articles      → search_vendor_kb (RAG corpus)
#   day1/data/kb_tickets/    12 IT support articles     → search_kb (RAG corpus)
#   day1/data/prompt*.md     system prompts             → utils.prompts fallback
#   day1/data/pdfs/          13 capability statements   → parse_vendor_pdf
#   day2/data/agent/         AGENTS.md + SKILL.md       → MemoryMiddleware / SkillsMiddleware
#   day2/data/wiki/          durable notes              → the wiki-memory lab
# Ignoring these would break every RAG lab and the Deep Agent. Keep the negations.
!day1/data/**/*.md
!day1/data/**/*.pdf
!day2/data/**/*.md
# …but NOT the wiki notes the agent writes at runtime. `open_questions.md` and `risk_signals.md` are
# seeded fixtures and ship; `wiki/vendors/` is generated output, and shipping a previous run's notes
# would mean Lab 02's "the agent starts cold" section is warm on a fresh clone.
day2/data/wiki/vendors/
# …and not the agent's scratch directory either. `!day2/data/**/*.md` above re-includes every .md
# under day2/data/, which silently swept up files the deep agent writes mid-run
# (day2/data/temp/quelmore_source_validation.md and friends). Output, not fixtures.
day2/data/temp/
!day3/data/**/*.md

# =============================================================================
# Local artifacts and authoring tooling — not workshop deliverables
# =============================================================================
# Agent output from earlier runs, machine-specific setup scripts, and prep tooling.
# Nothing in the labs or README references these; a `git add -A` swept them in once.
due_diligence_report_*.txt
# Prep/one-off shell scripts are authoring tooling, never part of the labs.
# Generic on purpose: naming the individual scripts would put internal filenames
# in a published file. No .sh file ships, so a blanket rule costs nothing.
*.sh
generate_agenda_pdf.py
# …and the fonts it embeds. Only that (internal) script reads them, so shipping ~8 MB of
# typefaces to the customer would be dead weight.
.fonts/
verify_rubric.py
iso_validation_report.txt

# Generated SQLite database — built from the committed JSON fixtures by
# day1/src/ticket_db.py:build_database(), which rebuilds whenever the JSON is newer. Committing the
# binary would let it drift silently from tickets.json/users.json, which are the source of truth.
# tickets.db IS committed: participants get a working database on clone with no build step, matching
# the reference workshop. It is byte-verified against tickets.json/users.json and its row text is
# scanned by the compliance gate, so it is not an unreadable blob in the published tree.
# `build_database()` still rebuilds it whenever either JSON is newer, so it cannot go stale.
!day1/data/tickets.db
day1/data/*.db-journal

# Delivery/presenter material — not workshop content.
*.html
*.pptx
*.png
generate_slides.py
utils/lc_brand.py

# Agent-written outputs — the labs regenerate these, so they must not ship.
# The two wiki seeds stay tracked so day2/data/wiki/ exists in a fresh clone;
# the agent's own notes underneath it do not.
# The Day 2 deep agent writes its finished reports here, into a directory that also holds one
# committed example. Ignore everything except that example: without this, a `git add -A` after a
# lab run commits agent output (the sweep produced
# `Final_Quelmore_Systems_Ltd_Due_Diligence_Report.md`, untracked and unignored).
day2/data/due_diligence_reports/*
!day2/data/due_diligence_reports/Quelmore_Systems_VND-001.md
day2/data/due_diligence/
day2/data/compliance_screening_results/

# Maintainer verification tooling — for whoever maintains this repo, not for participants.
# `verify_setup.py` DOES ship: a participant runs it before Lab 01 to prove their own credentials,
# fixtures and RAG index work, and the README tells them to. The other two are for whoever maintains
# this repo: verify_all.py scores the five dimensions and verify_execution.py drives the both-path
# sweep across all 17 labs. A participant has no use for either, and shipping them invites someone to
# run a 45-minute sweep against their own quota by accident.
#
# Their OUTPUT is excluded for a separate reason: sweep_results.txt records our machine's timings and
# file hashes, so shipping it would present our run as the customer's, and would make the content
# proof fail the moment they edit a lab.
verify_all.py
verify_execution.py
sweep_results.txt
.sweep_logs/
.env.sweep-backup

# Logs, wherever they land. `langgraph dev` writes them, the sweep writes them, and a stray one
# swept in by `git add -A` would ship workspace IDs and trace URLs to the customer.
*.log
*.log.*

# Internal prep docs — never publish (the blanket *.md rule above already covers
# these, but name them explicitly so the protection survives an edit to that rule).
workshop-agenda.md
AGENTS.md
VALIDATION.md


# MDA Lab
vendor-due-diligence/

